<div class="legal-deep-dive" style="margin-top: 30px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <h2 style="color: var(--primary);">Complete Legal Framework for Power of Attorney (PoA) in Property Deals</h2>
    <p>A <strong>Power of Attorney (PoA)</strong> is a formal legal instrument governed by the <strong>Powers of Attorney Act, 1882</strong> and Indian Stamp Act, 1899. It grants a designated agent (Attorney) authority to act on behalf of the principal in property management, sale execution, SRO registration, or court proceedings.</p>

    <h3>General Power of Attorney (GPA) vs. Special Power of Attorney (SPA)</h3>
    <p><strong>General Power of Attorney (GPA):</strong> Grants broad, multi-faceted operational powers to manage, rent, lease, or conduct legal affairs for multiple properties.</p>
    <p><strong>Special Power of Attorney (SPA):</strong> Restricted strictly to a single, specific act—such as presenting a specific Sale Deed before the Sub-Registrar or collecting an Encumbrance Certificate.</p>

    <h3>Supreme Court Ruling on PoA Property Sales (Suraj Lamp Case)</h3>
    <p>In the landmark judgment <em>Suraj Lamp & Industries Pvt Ltd vs State of Haryana (2011)</em>, the Supreme Court of India ruled that <strong>Power of Attorney is NOT an instrument of transfer</strong>. A property cannot be validly bought or sold through a GPA/SA/Will transfer. Real estate title transfers require a duly stamped and registered <strong>Sale Deed</strong> under Section 54 of the Transfer of Property Act.</p>

    <h3>Registration & Stamp Duty Mandates for PoA</h3>
    <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><strong>PoA Executed for Consideration (Deemed Sale):</strong> If a GPA authorizes an agent to sell property and receive consideration, states levy full Sale Deed stamp duty (5% to 8%) to curb stamp duty evasion.</li>
        <li><strong>PoA Granted to Blood Relations:</strong> Executing a PoA in favor of parents, spouse, children, or siblings attracts nominal fixed stamp duty (e.g., Rs. 500 to Rs. 2,000 depending on state SRO rules).</li>
        <li><strong>NRI Power of Attorney Execution:</strong> NRIs living abroad must execute the PoA at the Indian Embassy/Consulate, get it notarized, and subsequently get it <strong>adjudicated/embossed</strong> within 3 months of receipt in India at the local District Registrar Office (DRO).</li>
    </ul>
